    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-joseph-proudhon">Pierre-Joseph Proudhon</a> was the first political philosopher to call himself an anarchist and give the word a positive meaning; in early English usage it had stood for disorder.</li><li>Expelled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a> by the Marxists at the Hague Congress of 1872, the anarchists formed their own St. Imier International; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Bakunin</a> predicted that revolutionaries who took power on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>’s terms would become the workers’ new tyrants.</li><li>In the Spanish Revolution of 1936 an anarchist-inspired movement took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and collectivised the land across rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>; the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> closed the classical era of anarchism.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1901 the Catalan anarchist Francisco Ferrer founded the Escuela Moderna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, a secular school free of both state and church; it closed after constant harassment by the state, but inspired modern schools around the world.</p></aside>
    </section>

